-- title:
--   Level-zero edge chart: primes maximal or minimal
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring, $\pi \in \mathcal O$ an element whose principal ideal $(\pi)$ is maximal, and $r$ a natural number. Write $A :=$ `chartERing 𝒪 π r`, which by definition is the localisation away from the element `edgeQuot.discr 𝒪 π r` of the ring `edgeQuot 𝒪 π` $= \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\,2)\,\mathcal O / (\mathtt{edgeRel}\ \mathcal O\ \pi)$, the quotient of the polynomial ring in two variables $X_0, X_1$ over $\mathcal O$ by the principal ideal generated by the element `edgeRel 𝒪 π`, and where `edgeQuot.discr 𝒪 π r` is the class of the element `edgeDiscr 𝒪 r` of that polynomial ring. Let $A_0$ be the quotient of $A$ by the ideal generated by the single element $(\text{algebraMap}\ \pi)^{0+1}$, i.e. by the image of $\pi$ in $A$ raised to the power $0+1$ (the level-zero member of the family of levels $\pi^{n+1}$). The assertion is that every prime ideal $p$ of $A_0$ is either a maximal ideal of $A_0$ or a member of `minimalPrimes` $A_0$, that is, a minimal prime of $A_0$.
--
--   This is the dichotomy for primes of the special fibre of the standard edge chart in the Čerednik–Drinfeld/Mumford construction, at level zero; concretely, over the residue field $k = \mathcal O/(\pi)$ the chart becomes a localisation of $k[\xi,\eta]/(\xi\eta)$, a ring of Krull dimension at most one. It is used to supply the dimension clause in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.MumfordGlue.specialLevel_isField_isReduced_dim_infinite`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.MumfordGlue.specialLevel_isField_isReduced_dim_infinite).
